# AGENTS.md — {{.Name}}

A Go API service. Generated by Bogie (layout
{{.LayoutVersion}}, recorded in `bogie.toml`). Bogie is not a dependency:
nothing here imports it, and the code is yours.

**The layout is by role on purpose.** Do not "fix" it into idiomatic Go
layout. Underscored package names such as `admin_controllers`, one per
namespace, are deliberate; `.golangci.yml` disables ST1003 for exactly this
and nothing else.

## Commands

    make help           every task
    make up             postgres on :5440 (docker compose), healthy before it returns
    make db-prepare     create + migrate + seed
    make db-seed        apply db/seeds through the binary; safe to run again
    make credentials    edit the encrypted settings; ENV=production for that file
    make db-migrate     apply pending migrations; db-rollback, db-migrate-status, db-reset
    make g_migration_<name>   author a migration (go tool goose)
    make server         run the HTTP server (go run . serve); migrates at boot
    bogie server        the same, with live reload in development (Air)
    make test           go test -p 1 -race -cover ./...
    make lint           gofmt check, go vet, golangci-lint
    bin/ci              every check, run locally; green ends with gh signoff

`bogie` runs the same commands from any subdirectory, spelled with a colon for
tasks: `bogie db:prepare`, `db:migrate`, `db:rollback`, `db:seed`,
`credentials:edit -e production`, `server` (`s`), `test` (`t`). The tool adds the colon
spelling and nothing else; the app never imports it. In development
`bogie server` and `bogie worker` run under Air, so a saved file rebuilds
and restarts the process; `.air.toml` holds the settings and deleting it
turns reload off. `make server` never reloads, which is the thing to run
when you want one boot and one set of logs.{{if .Jobs}} The worker is a second
process in a second terminal: `bogie worker`, which reloads the same way.{{end}} A newer Bogie moves the
app to its layout with `bogie app:update`, a three-way merge of the files the
templates produce against the version `bogie.toml` records; your edits and
everything the generators wrote are kept, and a real overlap leaves conflict
markers.

CI runs here, not on a hosted runner: `bin/ci` is the whole pipeline, and a green run on a clean tree records a signoff
status on the commit. Run it before you push. Add a new check as one line at
the bottom of `bin/ci`.

## Configuration

Encrypted credentials, via `bogie-go/credentials`:
committed ciphertext, keys never committed, environment variables override.

    config/credentials.yml.enc            + config/master.key              development
    config/credentials/staging.yml.enc    + config/credentials/staging.key
    config/credentials/production.yml.enc + config/credentials/production.key

`make credentials` edits development's; `make credentials ENV=production`
edits that file with its own key. Every file is flat: groups at the top level
(`server:`, `database:`, and any `bogie g config` adds), and a key's
environment variable is its path, upper-cased: `server.addr` is overridden by
`SERVER_ADDR`, `database.url` by `DATABASE_URL`. There is no app prefix on
them, so a `DATABASE_URL` another app exports in your shell reaches this one
in development (test never reads it).

Two variables, deliberately separate: `{{.EnvPrefix}}_ENV` is a closed set,
`development`, `test`, `production`, and selects behaviour; staging is
`{{.EnvPrefix}}_ENV=production {{.EnvPrefix}}_CREDENTIALS=staging`, production
strictness with staging secrets. In a container the key arrives as
`{{.EnvPrefix}}_MASTER_KEY` and no key file exists. **Test never reads a
credentials file**: it runs from defaults and the environment, so tests need
nobody's secrets and can never open the development database.

## Layout

    main.go                      dispatches on the first argument: serve |{{if .Jobs}} worker |{{end}} migrate | db
    cli.go                       the migrate and db subcommands
    app/
      application.go             wiring: migrate at boot, config, connections, controllers
      controllers/
        application.go           root Server, one NAMED field per controller
        routes.go                the whole routing table
        public.go                no-route fallback: a file in public/, else public/404.html
        welcome.go               the development page at /; gone once a route claims /
        <name>_controller.go     one controller: a <Name>Controller, its routes, its actions
        <ns>_controllers/         one namespace: server.go · routes.go · <name>_controller.go …
      middlewares/               request id · logging · recover{{if .Jobs}}
      jobs/                      River workers, one file per job, registered above // bogie:jobs{{end}}
      services/<name>/           ctx first, knows nothing about HTTP
      services/database/         db create and drop, through the maintenance database
      services/migrate/          goose over the embedded migrations
      views/                     wire contracts; model structs never double as wire structs
      models/                    THE MODELS; imports nothing third-party
    config/                      credentials file + env → validated struct, no globals
      deploy.yml                 Kamal 2, staging; deploy.production.yml overlays it
    .kamal/secrets               what Kamal substitutes: the master key and the registry password
    Dockerfile                   one image, every role; keys never enter it (.dockerignore)
    db/
      db.go                      go:embed of migrate/*.sql
      migrate/                   goose SQL, timestamped; `bogie g model` writes the first
      seeds/                     seeds.go runs each file above // bogie:seeds, every
                                 time `db seed` runs; `bogie g seed` writes one
      sqlc/                      sqlc's generated queries (*.sql.go, make sqlc); never edited
      adapter/                   package adapter: the Store between app/models and sqlc
                                 (validation, row <-> models); `bogie g model` writes it
    public/                      404.html · 500.html, go:embedded
    lib/logger/
    test/                        what tests share: testdb (a clean test database),
                                 factories, testapp (the whole app), requests/

Tests live beside the code they test, in Ginkgo and Gomega, in
external `_test` packages. Each package with tests has one `<pkg>_suite_test.go`
that owns its shared setup (`app` in controllers, `store` in models). What
packages share (the test database, factories, the whole app) lives in `test/`.

## Deploy

Kamal 2. `config/deploy.yml` IS staging and a
bare `kamal deploy` targets it; `kamal deploy -d production` overlays
`config/deploy.production.yml`. Three things to fill in first: the servers,
the host names and the registry, marked CHANGE-ME. The image is one binary
in every role{{if .Jobs}}, and the `job` role is that image started as `{{.Name}} worker`{{end}};
it migrates at boot, so the database only has to exist. The encrypted
credentials travel in the image and the key arrives as `{{.EnvPrefix}}_MASTER_KEY`
from `.kamal/secrets`, which reads `config/credentials/staging.key`.
`make credentials ENV=staging` makes that file and key the first time.

## The six rules

1. `ctx context.Context` is the first parameter of anything doing I/O. No
   `context.TODO()`.
2. Interfaces are declared where they are CONSUMED, next to the code that uses
   them, holding only the methods it uses. Never exported from the package
   that implements them.
3. `app/models` imports nothing third-party.
4. Constructors return `(T, error)`. Only `main` exits.
5. `*gin.Context` never travels below `app/controllers`. A handler converts
   HTTP into model values, calls one function, and converts the result back.
6. No `util/`, `helpers/` or `common/`. The package name is a required prefix
   at every call site, so it has to say what it provides.

## Recipes

### Add a resource

    bogie g scaffold post title:string body:text

is a scaffold: the model (everything `g model` writes, below), the
wire shapes in `app/views/posts.go`, and `app/controllers/posts_controller.go`
with the five resourceful actions over the store, registered with the store
passed in. There is no service layer: the controller declares the slice of
the store it uses as an interface beside it (rule 2), and the store runs the
model's `Validate` on create and update, so validation runs on save and a bad
value is a 422 from the store. The model is named in the singular and the
views and controller in the plural. `bogie d scaffold
post` removes all of it. When a resource outgrows CRUD, `bogie g service`
gives the logic a home that knows nothing about HTTP.

### Add a controller

    bogie g controller comments index show create update destroy

One controller is one Go file, `app/controllers/comments_controller.go`
in the `controllers` package: a `CommentsController` type holding its
dependencies, `SetupRoutes`, and one method per action, with a request
test beside it. Name a resource in the plural: the name is used as typed,
so `comments` answers at `/comments` and `comment` at `/comment`. Go has no
autoload, so a controller exists only once it is constructed,
held and mounted. That is three one-line insertions, each above a marker
comment, and the generator makes all three, idempotently:

1. `app/controllers/application.go`, above `// bogie:controllers`: a named
   field, `Comments *CommentsController`.
2. `app/application.go`, above `// bogie:wire`: its construction,
   `server.Comments = controllers.NewCommentsController(log)`.
3. `app/controllers/routes.go`, above `// bogie:routes`: its mount,
   `s.Comments.SetupRoutes(&r.RouterGroup)`.

The actions are stubs answering 501 until you fill them in; index, show,
create, update and destroy get their resourceful routes, any other name is a
GET under that name, and new and edit are refused because nothing here renders
a form. Give the controller its dependencies as a small interface declared
beside it and pass the real thing on the wire line. `bogie d controller
comments` removes all of it, registration included. `bogie doctor` checks the
markers are intact and every controller is constructed and mounted. Never
delete a marker: the generators stop and name the file if one is missing;
they do not guess.

### Give a controller a dependency

A generated controller holds only a logger. To hand it a store or a service,
three edits, all in files you own:

1. Beside the controller, declare the slice it needs (rule 2), only the
   methods it calls:

        type commentsStore interface {
            GetComment(ctx context.Context, id string) (models.Comment, error)
        }

2. Add a field and a constructor parameter: `Store commentsStore`,
   `NewCommentsController(store commentsStore, log *slog.Logger)`.
3. On its wire line in `app/application.go`, pass the real thing:
   `server.Comments = controllers.NewCommentsController(store, log)`.
   `*models.Store` satisfies any interface made of its own methods.

A service's test hands in a fake with the same methods; it needs no database.
A controller's test does not: it goes through the wired `Server`. A
scaffolded controller shows the finished shape.

### Add a namespace

    bogie g controller admin/reports index show

A namespace (`admin`, as in `admin/reports`) is a Go
package: `app/controllers/admin_controllers/`, with `server.go` holding one
named field per controller above its own `// bogie:controllers`, and
`routes.go` mounting the `/admin` group above its own `// bogie:routes`.
Middleware every controller in the namespace shares, an auth check or a
shared secret, goes on that group, so the exposure boundary is visible in one
place. The first `g controller admin/...` writes the package and registers it
in the root with the same three lines a controller gets; each controller in
it is then `reports_controller.go` beside `server.go`, held by the namespace's
`Server`, mounted in the namespace's `routes.go`, and still constructed in
`app/application.go`: `server.Admin.Reports = admin_controllers.NewReportsController(log)`.
Dependencies never thread through the namespace. One level of namespace; a
version such as `v1` is a route group inside the namespace's `routes.go`.
`bogie d controller admin/reports` removes the controller, and the package
too once it holds no controller.

Only the controller gets a package; the model, the store and the views are flat,
the namespace in front of their names. `bogie g scaffold admin/pages title:string`
makes a model of its own, as Rails does: `admin_page`, table `admin_pages`,
`app/models/admin_page.go`, all flat, with `PagesController` in the admin
package at `/admin/pages`. To put an admin controller over a model that
exists, say `categories`, use `bogie g scaffold_controller admin/categories`:
it reads the model and writes only the controller, its spec and views of its own,
`app/views/admin_categories.go` with `AdminCategoryResponse`, so what staff see
can differ from the public shapes in `app/views/categories.go`.

{{if .Jobs}}### Add a background job

    bogie g job send_welcome

writes `app/jobs/send_welcome.go`, an Args type with a `Kind` and a Worker with
`Work(ctx, job)`, and registers it above `// bogie:jobs` in `app/jobs/jobs.go`.
Jobs run on River, on the same Postgres: enqueue with
`app.Jobs.Insert(ctx, jobs.SendWelcomeArgs{...}, nil)`, or inside a
transaction with `InsertTx`, so the job commits with the row or not at all.
`bogie worker` (or `make worker`) works the queue; it is its own process so a
backlog never slows a request. Args are stored as JSON: carry ids, not
records. River's schema is applied by `migrate`, not by db/migrate.

{{end}}### Add a setting

    bogie g config smtp address:req port:int=1025 username password:secret

adds a group to the credentials file: a typed struct in `config/smtp.go`,
registered in `config/config.go`, read as `s.Config.Smtp.Address`. Each key is
`name[:type][:flag][=value]`. Types are `string` (the default), `int`, `bool`
and `duration`. Flags are `req` (production refuses to boot without it) and
`secret` (the same, and the development credentials get a commented
placeholder, never an invented value). `=value` is what the development
credentials file gets. A key's environment variable is its path upper-cased:
`smtp.address` is `SMTP_ADDRESS`, and the environment wins over the file. There
is no app prefix, so a setting another app exports under the same name in your
shell reaches this one.

Values are written as a person would: `username=xxx` is `username: xxx`, bare,
and quoted only where YAML would read it as something else (`yes`, a number, a
colon, a space). A `bool` key takes `yes`/`no` too: `football:bool=yes` writes
`football: true`. `server` and `database` are the app's own groups: keys go
into their structs in `config/config.go` (`bogie g config database
username=xxx password=yyy`); the groups are never created or removed.

A flat key, with no group around it, has a value or a type and no group name:

    bogie g config support_email=help@example.com
    bogie g config retries:int:req

and is a field directly on `Config` (`s.Config.SupportEmail`), overridden by
`SUPPORT_EMAIL`. Its default goes in `defaults()` in `config/config.go`, by
hand. `bogie d config support_email` removes it. A bare word with no keys,
`bogie g config smtp`, is a mistake and refused.

Order: the top-level entries, groups and flat keys together, are kept
alphabetical, in the credentials file and in the `Config` struct, so a new one
is inserted where it sorts (before the first existing entry that sorts after
it, so a file already out of order is not reshuffled). The keys *inside* a
group are never sorted: they stay in the order you typed them.

Run it again to add keys to the group: they go above the markers in
`config/smtp.go`, and nothing that exists is overwritten, so the file's
`defaults` method, where per-environment policy goes by hand (Mailpit in
development, nothing in production), is safe. A key left without a value is
written commented out: a key present with an empty value would override the
default with nothing. One level of group only (`smtp.address`, not
`mail.smtp.address`), and `bogie d config smtp` removes the group and its lines in
`config.go`; its lines in the development credentials stay, harmless.

### Add authentication

    bogie g authentication secret      # a trusted service calls this one
    bogie g authentication token       # a user presents a bearer token
    bogie g authentication api_key     # a machine client presents a key

Each shape is a middleware on a route group. The
generator writes the middleware and its test in `app/middlewares`, a group
helper on the root Server in `app/controllers/authentication_<shape>.go`,
and the config: a group (`token:`, or `control:` for the secret shape) with a
`secret` key, as `bogie g config` writes it (see "Add a setting"), with a
random development value written into the credentials file so the app boots
as before. Staging and production get theirs with `make credentials ENV=...`,
and refuse to boot without. Then mount what the guard protects on the group instead of the
router, in `routes.go`:

    s.Posts.SetupRoutes(s.WithSecret(&r.RouterGroup))   // or WithUser, WithAPIKey

- **secret**: the `X-<App>-Secret` header (`middlewares.SecretHeader`),
  constant-time compared, Basic auth as a fallback for a browser. The
  caller sends one header, from its own credentials.
- **token**: JWT HS256 with `token.secret`. Another service mints a
  token for its user with any JWT library and this service verifies it;
  `middlewares.Subject(c)` is the subject in a handler. A service that signs
  users in itself mints with `s.Tokens().Mint(subject, ttl)`.
- **api_key**: an `api_keys` table with a bcrypt digest, a service, and
  `bogie api_keys:create NAME`, which prints the key once;
  `middlewares.APIKeyName(c)` says which key a request presented.

`bogie d authentication <shape>` removes it; the development value stays in
the credentials file, harmless.

### Add a service

    bogie g service publish_post

writes `app/services/publish_post/` with a `Service`, a `store` interface to
fill with only the methods it calls, a `Run(ctx)` to rename, and a test. It is
not wired anywhere: the controller that needs it declares what it wants. A
service is for logic that is not CRUD; a plain resource needs none.

### Add a model

    bogie g model comment body:text post:references score:integer

writes the `create_comments` migration, `db/queries/comments.sql` with get,
list, create, update and delete, `app/models/comment.go` with the type and an
empty `Validate`, `app/models/comment_test.go`, a spec for it that you add a
Context to for each rule, and `db/adapter/comments.go`, the store methods that convert
sqlc's row type to `models.Comment`, run `Validate` before create and update,
and map no-rows to `models.ErrNotFound`; then it runs sqlc. Types: string, text, integer, bigint, boolean, datetime,
uuid, jsonb, references; `:index` or `:uniq` after a type. `post:references`
is the column `post_id` with a foreign key and an index. Go names follow
sqlc's: `image_url` is `ImageUrl`, and only `id` is spelled `ID` (`PostID`). A `:uniq` column
also makes `Create`/`Update` map a duplicate value to `models.ErrInvalid`
("email is already taken"), not a raw database error; a `references` column
likewise makes them map a foreign key that names nothing to `models.ErrInvalid`
("post_id does not exist"). `Delete` maps a
foreign-key violation (a row something else still points at) to
`models.ErrInvalid` too ("post is still in use"), so a controller answers 422,
not 500.

Every generated column is NOT NULL with a type-appropriate default, so sqlc
emits plain Go types rather than pointers. A column that may be NULL says so,
`archived_at:datetime:null`, or is written so in a migration by hand; the model
then holds a pointer (`*string`, `*time.Time`), nil being NULL.

A column added to or dropped from a model that already exists is followed with
`bogie model:sync [TABLE]`, after the migration and `bogie db:migrate`. It
reads the columns from what sqlc makes of the migrations, adds the model's
field for each new column and removes the field for each dropped one, then
regenerates the queries, the store and the factory and merges them into your
copies, so `Validate` and every hand edit stay; the report has a line for each
file, and for each one it leaves alone. A hand edit on the same lines as the
change gets conflict markers to resolve. The views and controller are not
touched: add the field there if the API should carry it. It needs a clean git
tree.

This app's id scheme is `{{.IDScheme}}`, recorded in `bogie.toml` at `bogie
new` time: every `id` column and every `:references` foreign key is made of
it, so `models.Comment.ID` is {{if eq .IDScheme "uuid"}}a `string`{{else}}an
`int64`{{end}}. It is chosen once, app-wide — `bogie new NAME --id=uuid`
picks uuid instead of the bigint default — and cannot be mixed per model.

A hand-written addition to the store — a helper, a query wrapper, anything
beyond what the generator writes — goes in a sibling file in `db/adapter/`,
such as `db/adapter/comments_extra.go`, not inside the generated
`comments.go`. Go merges every file in a package, so it sees the same
`*Store` and `models.Comment` as the generated methods. Keeping it separate
means a later `bogie g model comment <col>:<type>` only ever reports
`conflict` on the file it would actually change; your sibling file is never
at risk, and there is nothing to pass `--force` over.

### Add a seed

    bogie g seed demo_posts

writes `db/seeds/demo_posts.go` with a `SeedDemoPosts(ctx, s *models.Store)
error` to fill in, and registers it above `// bogie:seeds` in
`db/seeds/seeds.go`. Unlike a migration, a seed carries no version table:
`db seed` (and `db prepare`) runs every seed every time, in the order
registered, stopping at the first error. That makes idempotency the seed's
own job — insert with `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`, or check before creating —
`bogie d
seed demo_posts` removes it, file and registration together.

### Change a table

    bogie g migration add_slug_to_posts slug:string:uniq
    bogie g migration remove_draft_from_posts draft:boolean

`create_<table>`, `add_<x>_to_<table>` and `remove_<x>_from_<table>` get their
SQL written; any other name gets an empty Up and Down to fill in. The
migrations are what sqlc compiles against, so the generator runs sqlc
immediately and `db/sqlc/*.sql.go` changes before the migration is ever
applied. `bogie db:migrate` applies it; `bogie db:rollback` checks the Down.
Queries go in `db/queries/<table>.sql`; `make sqlc` recompiles them, and a
wrong column name fails there, not in production. The generated files are
committed; `bin/ci` fails if they are stale.

The schema is owned here and applied at boot; there is no schema dump. A new
app has no table: `bogie g model` writes the first,
and `bin/ci` runs the database steps against whatever the migrations hold.

### Add a query

There is no `where` builder; a query is SQL with a name. sqlc compiles every
`*.sql` file in `db/queries/`, not just the one a model's own generator
wrote, so a hand-written query goes in a sibling file, such as
`db/queries/comments_extra.sql`, not inside the generated
`db/queries/comments.sql` — the same reason a hand-written store method
goes in `comments_extra.go` rather than `comments.go` ("Add a model", above):

    -- name: ListCommentsForPost :many
    SELECT * FROM comments WHERE post_id = $1 ORDER BY created_at DESC;

then `make sqlc`, which writes `ListCommentsForPost` into
`db/sqlc/comments.sql.go` with typed parameters, or fails if a column is
misspelled. Then a store method that calls it and converts the rows with
`commentFromRow`, as the generated methods do — written in
`db/adapter/comments_extra.go` too, not inside the generated
`comments.go`, so a later `g model comment <col>:<type>` never conflicts
with either sibling file. Annotations: `:one`, `:many`, `:exec`,
`:execrows`. The generated file is committed; `bin/ci` fails if it is
stale.

### Run a transaction

A write that touches more than one table, and must not half-succeed, goes
through `(*Store).withTx` — the one place `db/adapter/store.go` wraps a real
database transaction:

    func (s *Store) CreateOrderFromCart(ctx context.Context, userID, cartID int64) (models.Order, error) {
        var order models.Order
        err := s.withTx(ctx, func(tx pgx.Tx, q *sqlc.Queries) error {
            // every call here runs on the same transaction; an error
            // from any of them rolls all of them back, nothing commits
            return nil
        })
        return order, err
    }

written in a hand-written method's own sibling file, such as
`orders_extra.go`, the same place any other hand-written store method goes
("Add a model", above). `withTx` is unexported: it is for `db/adapter`
itself to call, never for a controller to reach past the store with.

### Static files and error pages

`public/` is a folder of plain files, a handful of them served
straight off disk, embedded into the binary rather than copied into the
image, no route needed. `app/controllers/routes.go` tries them, in order,
for any request nothing above claimed: a file in `public/` by its exact
path, then (development only) the welcome page at `/`, then
`public/404.html`. A panicking handler gets `public/500.html` the same way,
from `app/middlewares.Recover`.

To add a file — `favicon.ico`, `robots.txt` — drop it in `public/` and
widen the `//go:embed *.html` pattern in `public/public.go` to match its
extension; it is then served at its own path, no code change beyond that.
To change what 404 or 500 looks like, edit `public/404.html` or
`public/500.html` directly; both are plain, static HTML with no app name or
other value baked in, by the same rule that keeps `app/views` JSON-only.

### Write a test

Tests are **Ginkgo and Gomega** (`Describe`/`Context`/`It`, `Expect`), in
external `_test` packages (`package models_test`), beside the code. One suite
file per package, `<pkg>_suite_test.go`, runs the specs and owns the shared
setup as a package variable rebuilt before every spec:
`app` in `app/controllers` and `test/requests`, `store` in `db/adapter`. What
the suites share lives in `test/`:

- `test/testdb`: `testdb.Store(GinkgoT())` opens the TEST database, migrates it and
  truncates every table, so each spec starts from nothing; `testdb.Pool(t)`
  is a second connection for raw SQL. It needs Postgres up (`make up`) and
  never touches development data.
- `test/factories`: one function per model that builds a valid record,
  overrides only what the spec cares about, saves it through the real store
  and fails the test on error. `factories.Sequence("user")` keeps unique
  columns unique. Hand-written, no library.
- `test/testapp`: the whole app for a test, which the suite builds as `app`.
  `app.Get("/posts").Do().Status(200).JSON(&v)`, `.WithHeader(name, value)`
  on a request{{if .Jobs}}, and
  `app.Jobs("kind")` / `app.JobArgs("kind")` for what a request queued{{end}}.
- `app/controllers/*_controller_test.go`: one request test file beside each
  controller; `test/requests` keeps what spans controllers (a whole journey,
  wiring, failures).

Test **outside in**: the request test is the one every feature must have;
the store, model and service tests below it cover the edge cases the request
test would make tedious.

**How a spec reads.** `Describe` says what is under test; `Context` says the
situation, and is followed straight away by the `BeforeEach` that creates it;
`It` states one behavior and only acts and asserts.

```go
Describe("POST /sessions", func() {
    var user models.User

    Context("when the user exists", func() {
        BeforeEach(func() {
            user = factories.User(GinkgoT(), app.Store)
        })

        It("logs in with the right password", func() { ... })
        It("answers 401 for a wrong password", func() { ... })
    })

    Context("when the email is unknown", func() {
        It("answers 401, as it does for a wrong password", func() { ... })
    })
})
```

- A **request test** (beside the controller, in `package controllers_test`)
  sends HTTP through the real `Server`: its router, middleware, controllers
  and store, wired exactly as `serve` wires them, on the test database.
  Arrange with factories, assert on status and JSON. There is no test of a
  controller on its own with a fake store: the wiring is part of what is
  tested, and `test/requests/wiring_test.go` checks every controller is
  constructed and mounted.
- A **store test**, in `package models_test` so it can use the factories,
  uses `store`, which the suite resets before every spec.
- A **model test** is a plain function call: `Validate` and whatever rules
  live in `app/models`, nothing to set up.
- A **service test** fakes the store interface the service declared.
- To test something unexported, add an `export_test.go` in the package
  (`package services`) that exports it for the external test package only; it
  is compiled for tests and never shipped (see `app/controllers/export_test.go`).

`bogie test` runs everything with the race detector and coverage.

### When something is off

- `bogie doctor`: every marker exactly once, every controller constructed
  and mounted, tools pinned, layout files present.
- `make sqlc` after any change to `db/queries` or `db/migrate`; a stale
  `*.sql.go` fails `bin/ci`.
- A migration that will not roll back: `bogie db:rollback` runs the Down
  block, so write one.
- A 500 in a handler is logged with `request_id`; the response says
  "internal error" and nothing else, on purpose.
